  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Isolated showers and thunderstorms are\nexpected to continue across southeastern portions of Nevada this\nafternoon, and across eastern Nevada by tomorrow afternoon, as\nresidual moisture slowly pushes eastward. By mid-week, atmospheric\nstability will increase for a more calm weather conditions as the\nhigh pressure over the Four Corners weakens and moves east, while\nthe low pressure system stays north over southern Canada. Another\nstrong low pressure system from the Gulf of Alaska will make it's\nway towards the northwestern CONUS. This will extend into the \nsouthwestern CONUS, shifting the patterns to a southwesterly flow \nacross the state with much cooler temperatures moving in. Weather \nconditions remain dry as PWATs stay below 0.4 inches with some \nsouthwesterly afternoon breezes up to 15-20 mph, gusts up to 25-30\nmph. High temperatures will be several degrees below normal in \nthe 70s to 80s. \n\nBy the weekend, an interesting weather pattern takes shape with\nmodels agreeing that the low pressure system to the northwest\npushes further south along the Pacific northwest, strengthens, \nand situates over Oregon and Washington. At the same time, a \nPacific tropical storm will move north along Mexico's coastline \nand into the southwestern US, bringing a surge of tropical \nmoisture into Nevada. As for the timing and position, by the end \nof the weekend the model discrepancies begin to increase, but \nwith little deviations among them. Both consistently agree on the \nmoisture surge and timing, but slight differentiate on the \nposition. GFS pushes towards Southern California, while ECWMF \nextends a little further outward off the Coast of California then \npushes into northern California. The low pressure to the northwest\nis also showing some deviations within the models with GFS \nshowing the system weakens and pushes east as the tropical system \nmoves in, while ECMWF pushes further out into the Pacific and \nmerges with the tropical system. These systems will be closely \nlooked at as we approach into the next weekend.",
